 ok   G8  THE GENERATIONS FIGURE — what a before/after drive snapshot structurally cannot take. For each tracked write target, the number of DISTINCT committed blobs the path has carried. His §3 says he "restored both", and he did — to the content committed before his fire. This leg asks whether that content was itself a re-stamp, which is a question about history and not about a working tree. A recording whose committed content has been rewritten N times is a record of its last drive, not of the round it is named for, and a `git checkout` cannot recover what was never committed. The leg reddens if nothing is multi-generation, because then the hazard is working-tree-only and his cure is sufficient
